  "discussions_data": [
    {
      "id": "1f7f87e32b3234e5f4cd5e7f27fe6e6571424c7c",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 681429903,
          "type": null,
          "body": "(taking the discussion from !4214) over here)\n\n@jvalverde I agree that this isn't the best heuristic starting _ab initio_ since it is not very intuitive, but it does seem to be the current behavior. I've checked a few examples and that is how it works - unquoted strings are interpreted as field/protocol name first, then a byte array if that's possible (either case acceptable for the alphabetic hex digits), but fall back to strings otherwise.\n\nThis would completely remove the matching as protocol name when on the RHS, where protocol names are impossible. The counterintuitive part already exists outside of the protocol name matching.\n\nIt is possible to use byte arrays that have several bytes, so I'm not sure that special-casing strings shorter than 3 bytes prevents more confusion than it causes.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
